.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-volume-read-slices 1  "tapectl-volume-read-slices " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-volume\-read\-slices \- Read encrypted slices from a volume into staging (then use `volume write` to write them)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl volume read\-slices\fR <\fB\-\-from\fR> [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] <\fB\-\-unit\fR> [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Read encrypted slices from a volume into staging (then use `volume write` to write them)
.PP
Reads the unit\*(Aqs slices off the loaded tape, checks each against its recorded hash, and records where it put them, so `volume write` can write them to another cartridge without the source directory. Every version of the unit on the volume is read except those marked reclaimable or purged; `\-\-version` reads one version, whatever its state. The staging directory is checked for room before the first slice is read, and a read that does not finish removes what it wrote.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-from\fR \fI<FROM>\fR
Source volume label
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes. Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it
.TP
\fB\-\-unit\fR \fI<UNIT>\fR
Unit name to read
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR \fI<N>\fR
Read only this version of the unit (a reclaimable or purged version is read when named). Without it, every version on the volume except the reclaimable and purged ones is read
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
Tape device (by\-id path). Defaults to the only configured drive; required when more than one is configured
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr. Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 confirmation prompts. It never reaches a Tier\-3 refusal — those are facts, not risks to accept
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file.

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs.

Defaults to ~/.tapectl. The config file is taken from <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config overrides it. Also settable as TAPECTL_HOME.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
